# Visualisation standard

Figures are a primary scientific output here. Treat each one as a claim.

## Library choice

| Use | Library |
| --- | --- |
| Trajectories, phase portraits, transition graphs, recurrence plots, anything a reader would want to hover/zoom | **Plotly** |
| Print panels for the manuscript: distributions, regressions, faceted comparisons, correlation matrices | **Seaborn / Matplotlib** |

Both draw from `viz/theme.py`. Never set colours, fonts or sizes ad hoc in a
figure function — extend the theme instead.

## Mandatory export bundle

`viz.export.save_figure()` writes, for figure `name`:

```
<name>.html            interactive (plotly only)
<name>.png             200 dpi, white background, publication size
<name>.svg             vector
<name>.data.parquet    the exact tidy frame that was plotted
<name>.meta.json       {run_ids, git_sha, config_sha, caption, alt_text, units, generated_at}
```

A figure without its `.data.parquet` is not an artifact. If the plotted object
is not naturally tabular (e.g. a recurrence matrix), store it as `.data.npz`
and say so in the metadata.

## Design requirements

- Colour-blind-safe qualitative palette; perceptually uniform sequential maps
  (`viridis`/`magma`); diverging maps only for genuinely signed quantities,
  always centred at zero.
- Axis labels carry units: `lag τ (generator tokens)`, not `tau`. Where an axis
  is naturally measured in window turnovers, show **both** `tokens` and `T/W`.
- Uncertainty is not optional: bootstrap CI bands over trajectories, error bars
  over seeds. A curve averaged over trajectories without a spread band is
  incomplete.
- Log axes for power laws (MSD, timescales), with the fitted exponent and its
  CI annotated on the plot.
- Mark the **context horizon** (`t = W`) on every time-axis figure — it is the
  central landmark of this paper.
- Distinguish per-trajectory traces (thin, low alpha) from the ensemble summary
  (thick). Never show only the mean.
- Every 2-D embedding projection (UMAP/t-SNE/PCA) must carry the words
  "illustration only; all statistics computed in the full space" in its
  caption, and must not be the basis of any claim.

## Captions

Written at figure-creation time, stored in `.meta.json`, reused verbatim in the
stage report and manuscript. A caption states: what is plotted, over what data
(models/W/temperature/n trajectories), what the reader should conclude, and
what the figure cannot establish.

## Tables

`reporting/tables.py` emits every table in four forms: `.csv` (canonical),
`.parquet` (typed), `.md` (readable in the report), `.html` (styled). Numbers
carry uncertainty and explicit units; report `n` for every aggregate.
